"""English: extra explanations on calculator pages · Regression part 2 (robust to Tobit, 16 methods)."""
from .metode_extra_en import XE

_ROB_DATA = ["A Y column and one or more numeric X columns.",
             "Do not delete outliers beforehand; let the robust method decide their weights.",
             "Re-examine observations that receive small weights: a recording error, different units, or a special case?"]

XE("regresi-robust-m-estimator-huber",
   "House price data contain a few transactions far above market, for example because the seller was in a hurry or a zero was mistyped. With OLS, these points pull the regression line and change the conclusions. The Huber estimator gives full weight to ordinary observations and down-weights those with large residuals, so the line better represents the bulk of the data.",
   _ROB_DATA,
   [("Coefficients", "Interpreted as in linear regression, but less affected by outliers in the Y direction."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "A large gap between Huber and OLS coefficients signals strongly influential observations."),
    ("Outlier diagnostics", "Scaled residuals and final weights of each observation. Weight 1 means treated as in OLS; a small weight means its influence was reduced."),
    ("Plot", "The Huber and OLS lines on one chart; low-weight points are marked.")],
   ["Thinking Huber protects against high-leverage points (extreme X values); use LTS or MM for that.",
    "Deleting all low-weight observations and running OLS again.",
    "Not reporting that robust estimation was used and why."],
   "Example wording: “Because of several extreme transactions, Huber M-estimation (c = 1.345) was used. Floor area significantly affected price (b = 4.1 million/m²; P < 0.001), smaller than the OLS estimate (5.3 million/m²), which was pulled by three extreme transactions.”",
   [("What does the constant 1.345 mean?", "The scaled residual beyond which weights start to drop; this value gives 95% efficiency if the errors really are normal."),
    ("When is Huber enough, and when is another method needed?", "Huber is enough for outliers in Y. If there are suspicious extreme X values or very many outliers, use LTS or bisquare.")])

XE("regresi-robust-tukey-bisquare",
   "In laboratory data, a few samples are contaminated so their values are far from the pattern. Huber still gives those samples some influence. Tukey’s bisquare lowers weights smoothly and gives zero weight to very large residuals, so clearly deviant observations have no effect on the line at all.",
   _ROB_DATA,
   [("Coefficients", "Interpreted as in linear regression; often almost the same as OLS without the outliers."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "Shows how far outliers shifted the OLS estimate."),
    ("Outlier diagnostics", "Zero weight marks observations that are effectively set aside; weights between 0 and 1 show partly suspicious observations."),
    ("Plot", "The bisquare line compared with OLS.")],
   ["Starting bisquare from OLS values when outliers are very large; the calculator uses robust starting values to avoid this.",
    "Taking zero weight as proof that the data are wrong; check the context first.",
    "Using bisquare on small data with many outliers without comparing it with other methods."],
   "Example wording: “Tukey bisquare robust regression (c = 4.685) gave zero weight to two contaminated samples. The calibration slope was 0.982 (SE 0.011), whereas OLS gave 1.124.”",
   [("How does it differ from Huber?", "Huber still gives large outliers bounded influence; bisquare removes their influence entirely beyond c."),
    ("What is an MM-estimator?", "A combination of a highly robust start (an S-estimator) with an efficient bisquare step; widely regarded as the best choice for robust regression.")])

XE("regresi-robust-least-trimmed-squares-lts",
   "Household survey data include a group of respondents from a very different area, so almost a third of the data follow another pattern and some have extreme X values. M-methods are not strong enough for this. LTS finds the line that best fits roughly the best half of the data, so it stays correct even when nearly half the data deviate.",
   _ROB_DATA,
   [("Coefficients", "From the retained set of observations (usually after reweighting)."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "A large difference signals that OLS is dominated by a group of deviant observations."),
    ("Outlier diagnostics", "Trimmed observations and their scaled residuals. The outlier map separates vertical outliers, good leverage points, and bad leverage points."),
    ("Plot", "LTS and OLS lines, with trimmed points marked.")],
   ["Reporting raw LTS standard errors; its efficiency is low, so it is usually followed by a reweighting step.",
    "Treating good leverage points as nuisances; they follow the pattern and actually add precision.",
    "Results may change slightly between runs because the algorithm uses random subsets; report it if that happens."],
   "Example wording: “To deal with a group of deviant observations, LTS regression (h = 0.5n) followed by reweighting was used. Fourteen of 120 households were identified as outliers; the income coefficient changed from 0.12 (OLS) to 0.31 (reweighted LTS).”",
   [("What does a 50% breakdown point mean?", "Almost half the data may be arbitrarily wrong without making the estimate unbounded; OLS breaks down with a single point."),
    ("Why can results differ slightly on each run?", "The FAST-LTS algorithm uses many random subsets; with clear data, results are practically stable.")])

XE("regresi-kernel",
   "The relationship between air temperature and daily electricity use is neither a line nor a parabola: it rises when cold (heating), falls at moderate temperatures, then rises again when hot (cooling). Kernel regression estimates the curve directly from the data with a weighted average around each point, without specifying a functional form first.",
   ["Numeric X and Y columns; ideally a fair number of observations (dozens or more).",
    "Choose the kernel (Gaussian, Epanechnikov, and others) and the local degree (0 = Nadaraya–Watson, 1 = local linear).",
    "Let the bandwidth be chosen automatically by cross-validation, or enter it yourself in X units."],
   [("Bandwidth comparison", "Cross-validation error for several bandwidths; a small bandwidth makes the curve jagged, a large one makes it too smooth."),
    ("Plot", "The fitted curve over the scatterplot; watch the edges, where the estimate is less certain."),
    ("Observed and predicted values", "Residuals should show no pattern against X."),
    ("Interpretation", "A sentence summary of the shape of the relationship (rising, falling, turning points).")],
   ["Using degree 0 (Nadaraya–Watson) and being surprised by bending at the edges; local linear reduces boundary bias.",
    "Choosing the bandwidth by hand so the curve “looks nice”.",
    "Using the kernel curve to predict outside the data range."],
   "Example wording: “Local linear kernel regression (Gaussian kernel, bandwidth 2.1 °C chosen by cross-validation) showed minimum electricity use at about 24 °C, rising sharply above 30 °C.”",
   [("Does the kernel type matter?", "Far less than the bandwidth; different kernels with suitable bandwidths give almost the same curve."),
    ("What about many predictors?", "Kernel regression quickly loses precision in many dimensions; use an additive GAM.")])

XE("loess-lowess",
   "A researcher wants to see the trend in pollutant levels over 10 years without being distracted by daily fluctuations and a few extreme values from fires. LOESS fits a local line around each point with tricube weights, and the LOWESS version adds robustness iterations so extreme values do not pull the curve.",
   ["Numeric X and Y columns.",
    "The span (share of data per local window) can be chosen automatically by cross-validation.",
    "Tick robustness iterations (LOWESS) if there are outliers."],
   [("Span comparison", "Cross-validation error for several spans; a small span is lively, a large span smooth."),
    ("Plot", "The LOESS curve over the scatterplot, with a confidence band when available."),
    ("Observed and predicted values", "Residuals for checking patterns not yet captured."),
    ("Interpretation", "A summary of the direction and changes in the trend.")],
   ["Treating the LOESS curve as a model that can be written as one formula.",
    "Using a very small span so noise is read as pattern.",
    "Skipping robustness iterations when there are extreme values."],
   "Example wording: “The LOWESS curve (span 0.35; two robustness iterations) showed PM2.5 levels were fairly stable until 2019, then fell by about 18% to 2023.”",
   [("Degree 1 or 2?", "Degree 1 is more stable at the edges; degree 2 is better for curves with sharp peaks or troughs."),
    ("How do LOESS and LOWESS differ?", "LOWESS is Cleveland’s original version with robustness iterations; LOESS extends it to degree 2 and several predictors.")])

XE("regresi-spline",
   "Children’s height grows fast in infancy, slows in childhood, then surges again at puberty. A single polynomial struggles to follow such changes. A spline joins polynomial pieces at several knots so the curve is flexible yet smooth, and can still be analysed with ordinary regression tools.",
   ["Numeric X and Y columns.",
    "Choose the spline type (natural or cubic); natural splines are more stable at the edges.",
    "Let the number of knots be chosen automatically, or set it yourself (3–5 knots suit most data)."],
   [("Choosing the number of knots", "AIC or cross-validation error for several numbers of knots; pick the smallest, or the simplest with almost the same result."),
    ("Knot positions", "Usually at quantiles of X, so each segment contains enough data."),
    ("Plot", "The spline curve with confidence band; interpret the shape of the curve, not the basis coefficients."),
    ("Observed and predicted values", "Residuals for checking fit.")],
   ["Using too many knots so the curve follows noise.",
    "Interpreting spline basis coefficients one by one.",
    "Using an ordinary cubic spline that runs wild at the ends, when a natural spline is more stable."],
   "Example wording: “The relationship between age and height was modelled with a natural cubic spline with four knots at age quantiles. The spline model outperformed the linear model (F test: P < 0.001) and showed a growth spurt at ages 11–14.”",
   [("Spline or polynomial?", "A spline is usually better because its flexibility is local: a change in one part of the curve does not affect other parts."),
    ("How do I test whether the relationship is linear?", "Compare the spline model with the linear model using an F test or AIC.")])

XE("regresi-kuantil",
   "An education researcher suspects tutoring helps low-achieving students more than high-achieving ones. Ordinary linear regression only answers the effect on the mean. Quantile regression estimates effects at the 10th percentile, the median, the 90th percentile, and so on, revealing how the effect differs across the whole score distribution.",
   ["A Y column and one or more numeric X columns.",
    "Tick the quantiles to estimate; 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, and 0.9 are common choices.",
    "Bootstrap replicates set the precision of the standard errors; 100–200 is usually enough."],
   [("Coefficients per quantile", "The change in the Y quantile per unit of X. Coefficients that differ across quantiles show that the effect of X is not uniform."),
    ("Bootstrap standard errors", "Computed by resampling data pairs; no normality assumption needed."),
    ("Coefficient plot", "Coefficients as a function of τ compared with the OLS coefficient; a sloping line means the effect differs across quantiles."),
    ("Observed and predicted values (median)", "Fit of the median line to the data.")],
   ["Interpreting the 90th-percentile coefficient as the effect on “the best students”; it is the effect on the conditional quantile of Y, not on particular individuals.",
    "Estimating extreme quantiles (0.01 or 0.99) with little data.",
    "Not checking for crossing quantile lines."],
   "Example wording: “Quantile regression showed a larger tutoring effect at the lower quantiles of exam scores (τ = 0.1: b = 8.4; SE 1.9) than at the upper quantiles (τ = 0.9: b = 2.1; SE 1.5), whereas OLS gave a single average effect of 5.0.”",
   [("What are the advantages of quantile regression?", "It resists outliers in Y, needs no constant variance, and shows effects across the whole distribution, not just the mean."),
    ("Why use bootstrap standard errors?", "The asymptotic formula depends on the error density at that quantile, which is hard to estimate; the bootstrap avoids the problem.")])

XE("generalized-additive-model-gam",
   "An ecologist models fish abundance from water temperature, depth, and salinity. The temperature effect is clearly curved (there is an optimum), depth looks linear, and the shape of the salinity effect is unknown. A GAM lets each predictor have its own curve shaped by the data, while still adding them up in one interpretable model.",
   ["A numeric Y column.",
    "Choose which predictors get a smooth effect s(X) and which stay linear.",
    "Basis size per smooth sets the maximum flexibility; the default is usually enough, increase it if the edf approaches the limit."],
   [("Smooth terms", "Effective degrees of freedom (edf) and a test for each curve. An edf around 1 means a practically linear effect; a large edf means a wiggly curve."),
    ("Linear terms", "Coefficients of linear predictors, interpreted as in ordinary regression."),
    ("GAM vs linear regression", "Comparison of AIC, R², and error; shows whether the extra flexibility is worthwhile."),
    ("Partial effect plots", "The shape of each predictor’s curve with confidence band; this is the main way to interpret a GAM.")],
   ["Giving every predictor a smooth effect without reason, making the model too flexible.",
    "Treating P-values of smooth terms as exact; they are approximate.",
    "Ignoring concurvity, where the curves of predictors explain each other."],
   "Example wording: “The GAM showed a nonlinear effect of temperature on fish abundance (edf = 3.6; P < 0.001) with an optimum around 27 °C, while depth had a negative linear effect (b = −0.04 per metre; P = 0.01). The model explained 64% of the deviance.”",
   [("What does edf mean?", "A measure of curve flexibility. An edf of 1 is a straight line; an edf of 4 is roughly as flexible as a degree-4 polynomial."),
    ("Can a GAM handle binary or count responses?", "Yes, with a suitable distribution and link function, as in a GLM. This calculator handles a continuous numeric response.")])

XE("regresi-data-panel",
   "An economist observes 34 provinces over 10 years to see how education spending affects the human development index. Each province has fixed unmeasured traits such as culture or history. Panel data regression uses the repeated observations to control for those fixed traits.",
   ["Long format: one row per unit per period, with entity, time, Y, and X columns.",
    "A balanced panel (every unit in every period) is easiest, but unbalanced panels can still be analysed.",
    "Variables that do not change over time cannot be estimated in a fixed effects model."],
   [("Model selection tests", "The Chow test (common versus fixed effect), the Breusch–Pagan LM test (common versus random), and the Hausman test (fixed versus random). Together they decide which model to report."),
    ("Common, fixed, and random effects", "Coefficients from the three models; a large gap between common and fixed signals bias from unmeasured unit traits."),
    ("Coefficient comparison", "A side-by-side summary of the three models."),
    ("Individual effects (FE)", "Each unit’s own intercept; shows which units have high or low baseline levels.")],
   ["Using the common effect (pooled OLS) without testing for unit effects.",
    "Choosing random effects when the Hausman test is significant.",
    "Not using standard errors clustered by unit."],
   "Example wording: “The Chow test (P < 0.001) and the Hausman test (χ² = 14.2; P = 0.003) indicated the fixed effects model was most appropriate. Each 1% increase in education spending per capita was associated with a 0.08-point increase in HDI (P = 0.004).”",
   [("What is the difference between fixed and random effects?", "Fixed effects use only within-unit variation and are safe when unit traits are correlated with X; random effects are more efficient but biased if that correlation exists."),
    ("Why do variables like land area disappear in the FE model?", "Because they do not change over time, so they are absorbed into each unit’s fixed effect.")])

XE("regresi-deret-waktu-arimax",
   "A company wants to measure the effect of monthly advertising spend on sales. This month’s sales also depend on last month’s, so ordinary regression errors are correlated and t tests become too optimistic. ARIMAX combines regression on advertising with an ARIMA model for the errors.",
   ["A time column (for ordering), a Y column, and one or more exogenous X columns.",
    "Data must be in order with no missing periods.",
    "Set the AR order (p), differencing (d), and MA order (q); start with a simple model such as (1, 0, 0)."],
   [("Coefficients", "The effect of X on Y after accounting for the error autocorrelation structure, along with AR and MA coefficients."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "OLS standard errors are usually too small when errors are positively autocorrelated; compare the two."),
    ("Residual autocorrelation", "Residual ACF and the Ljung–Box test; a good model leaves uncorrelated residuals."),
    ("Plot", "Observed data and fitted values over time.")],
   ["Regressing two series that both keep rising without differencing (spurious regression).",
    "Forgetting that forecasting requires future values of X.",
    "Choosing ARIMA orders that are too large for a short series."],
   "Example wording: “Regression with AR(1) errors (φ = 0.62) showed that each additional Rp10 million of advertising increased sales by Rp38 million (SE 12 million; P = 0.002). Residuals were not autocorrelated (Ljung–Box Q(12) = 9.8; P = 0.63).”",
   [("What does d = 1 mean?", "The model is fitted to period-to-period differences, needed when the series is non-stationary (for example keeps rising)."),
    ("How do I choose p and q?", "Look at the ACF and PACF of the OLS residuals, then compare several models with AIC.")])

XE("regresi-spasial-sar-lag",
   "Land prices in one sub-district depend on land prices in surrounding sub-districts: development in one area raises prices next door. The spatial lag model (SAR) includes the neighbours’ average response as a predictor, so spillovers between areas can be measured.",
   ["Coordinate columns (longitude and latitude), a Y column, and X columns.",
    "Choose the spatial weights matrix: k nearest neighbours or distance-based.",
    "Each row represents one area; make sure no coordinates are duplicated."],
   [("Spatial autocorrelation tests", "Moran’s I on OLS residuals and LM tests; show whether a spatial model is needed and which kind (lag or error)."),
    ("SAR coefficients", "ρ measures the strength of dependence between neighbours’ responses. β coefficients cannot be read directly as marginal effects."),
    ("Direct, indirect, total effects", "Direct: the effect of X in an area on that area itself; indirect: spillover to other areas; total: their sum."),
    ("OLS coefficients (benchmark)", "Usually biased under a SAR model because they ignore spatial dependence.")],
   ["Interpreting β as a marginal effect.",
    "Choosing a lag model without looking at the LM tests; if only LM-error is significant, a spatial error model is more appropriate.",
    "Using a weights matrix that makes no sense for the study area."],
   "Example wording: “The robust LM-lag test was significant (P = 0.004), so a SAR model was used (ρ = 0.41; P < 0.001). The total effect of distance to the city centre was −2.3 million/m² per km, made up of a direct effect of −1.5 and a spillover of −0.8.”",
   [("Why is β not a marginal effect?", "A change in X spreads to neighbours through ρ and comes back; the real effect is summarised by the direct and indirect effects."),
    ("How many neighbours k is right?", "Usually 4–8. Try several values and make sure the conclusions do not change drastically.")])

XE("regresi-spasial-sem-error",
   "Rice yield per district depends on fertiliser and irrigation, but also on unmeasured factors such as soil type and microclimate that are spatially patterned. As a result, regression errors of neighbouring districts are correlated. The spatial error model (SEM) handles that correlation so standard errors and tests are correct.",
   ["Coordinate columns (longitude and latitude), a Y column, and X columns.",
    "Choose the spatial weights matrix: k nearest neighbours or distance-based.",
    "One row per area."],
   [("Spatial autocorrelation tests", "Moran’s I of OLS residuals and the LM-error and LM-lag tests; SEM is chosen when (robust) LM-error is significant."),
    ("SEM coefficients", "λ measures the spatial correlation of the errors. β is interpreted as a marginal effect, as in OLS."),
    ("OLS coefficients (benchmark)", "Usually similar to SEM, but with standard errors that are too small.")],
   ["Assuming spatially correlated errors do not matter because OLS coefficients stay unbiased; standard errors and tests become wrong.",
    "Choosing SEM when it is the robust LM-lag test that is significant.",
    "Concluding there is no spatial pattern from Moran’s I with few areas; the test has little power."],
   "Example wording: “OLS residuals showed spatial autocorrelation (Moran’s I = 0.32; P < 0.001), and only the robust LM-error test was significant. The spatial error model (λ = 0.48) showed N fertiliser significantly affected yield (b = 0.012 t/kg; SE 0.004).”",
   [("When SEM, when SAR?", "SEM when dependence comes from spatially patterned unmeasured factors; SAR when an area’s response genuinely affects its neighbours. LM tests help choose."),
    ("What if both LM tests are significant?", "Consider the spatial Durbin model (SDM) or a model containing both.")])

XE("geographically-weighted-regression-gwr",
   "The effect of education on income may be strong in big cities but weak in rural areas. Global regression gives only one average number for the whole region. GWR estimates a local regression at each location with weights that fall with distance, producing a map of coefficients that shows where the effect is strong or weak.",
   ["Coordinate columns (longitude and latitude), a Y column, and X columns.",
    "There should be a fair number of locations (dozens to hundreds).",
    "Let the bandwidth be chosen automatically by cross-validation, or set the number of nearest neighbours."],
   [("Summary of local coefficients", "Minimum, quartiles, median, and maximum of each coefficient across locations; a wide range signals relationships that vary between areas."),
    ("GWR vs OLS", "AICc and R² of both models; a GWR with much smaller AICc signals real spatial variation."),
    ("Coefficients per location", "Local coefficient values for mapping."),
    ("Plot", "A map or chart of local coefficients.")],
   ["Treating every map pattern as real without testing coefficient variation.",
    "Running hundreds of local tests without a multiple-testing correction.",
    "Using a very small bandwidth so each local regression contains only a handful of points."],
   "Example wording: “GWR (adaptive bandwidth of 42 neighbours) outperformed OLS (AICc 812 versus 851). The years-of-schooling coefficient ranged from 0.6 to 2.3 million rupiah per year, highest in urban areas.”",
   [("When is GWR appropriate?", "When there is good reason to think relationships differ between areas and the aim is to explore spatial patterns."),
    ("What is an adaptive bandwidth?", "A fixed number of nearest neighbours, so the window widens where points are sparse and narrows where they are dense.")])

XE("mixed-effect-model",
   "Maths scores of 600 students from 25 schools are related to study hours. Students from the same school tend to be similar, so observations are not independent. A mixed-effects model gives each school a random intercept (and a random slope if needed), so standard errors are correct and variation between schools can be measured.",
   ["Long format: one row per observation, with group, Y, and X columns.",
    "There should be enough groups (ideally 10 or more) to estimate the between-group variance.",
    "Choose a random slope only for predictors whose effect is genuinely expected to differ between groups."],
   [("Fixed effects", "Population-average coefficients, interpreted as in ordinary regression."),
    ("Variance components and ICC", "Between-group and within-group variance; the ICC is the share of variation due to groups."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "OLS standard errors are usually too small because they ignore clustering."),
    ("BLUP per group", "Predicted random effect of each group, pulled towards the overall mean; small groups are pulled more strongly.")],
   ["Ignoring the clustering and using ordinary regression.",
    "Adding random slopes for many predictors with few groups, so the model fails to converge.",
    "Comparing models with different fixed effects using REML."],
   "Example wording: “A mixed-effects model with a random intercept for school showed that each additional weekly study hour increased scores by 1.8 points (SE 0.3; P < 0.001). Twenty-two per cent of the variation in scores came from differences between schools (ICC = 0.22).”",
   [("Fixed or random effects for schools?", "Random if schools are seen as a sample from a population of schools and the aim is to generalise; fixed if interest is only in those particular schools."),
    ("Why do BLUPs differ from group means?", "Because BLUPs borrow information from all groups: the means of small or noisy groups are pulled towards the overall mean.")])

XE("regresi-cox-survival",
   "A clinical trial compares the survival of cancer patients on two therapies, taking age and stage into account. Some patients are still alive when the study ends (censored). Cox regression uses the information from censored patients and produces hazard ratios without needing to specify the shape of the baseline hazard.",
   ["A column for time to event or censoring, and a status column (1 = event, 0 = censored).",
    "Numeric and categorical covariates.",
    "Make sure time units are consistent, and that censoring is unrelated to prognosis."],
   [("Coefficients and hazard ratios", "HR = e^β. An HR of 0.6 means a 40% lower event rate at any time; an HR of 1.5 means 50% higher."),
    ("Proportional hazards test", "A Schoenfeld residual test; a small P means the hazard ratio changes over time and the assumption is violated for that covariate."),
    ("Baseline cumulative hazard", "Used to build fitted survival curves for a given covariate combination."),
    ("Plot", "Survival curves by group; crossing curves signal an assumption problem.")],
   ["Deleting censored patients or treating them as having the event.",
    "Ignoring the proportional hazards test.",
    "Interpreting the HR as the ratio of the overall probability of dying; the HR is a ratio of instantaneous rates."],
   "Example wording: “After controlling for age and stage, therapy B reduced the hazard of death by 38% compared with therapy A (HR = 0.62; 95% CI: 0.45–0.86; P = 0.004). The proportional hazards assumption held (global test P = 0.41).”",
   [("Why does the baseline hazard not need to be specified?", "Because in Cox’s partial likelihood the baseline hazard appears in the numerator and denominator and cancels."),
    ("What if the PH assumption is violated?", "Stratify by that variable, add an interaction with time, or use an accelerated failure time model.")])

XE("regresi-tobit",
   "A survey measures household spending on cigarettes; many households spend nothing (zero) and the rest vary. Linear regression on these data is biased by the pile of zeros. The Tobit model assumes a latent propensity that is observed only when positive, and estimates the effect of income while accounting for this censoring.",
   ["Numeric Y and X columns.",
    "Tick left censoring and enter the lower limit (usually 0); right censoring is used when there is an upper limit, for example a maximum exam score.",
    "Units at the limit must remain in the data; Tobit is not for truncated data."],
   [("Tobit coefficients", "The effect of X on the latent variable; this is an upper bound for the effect on observed Y."),
    ("Marginal effects", "The effect on the mean of observed Y (β times the probability of not being censored); this is the most policy-relevant number."),
    ("Comparison with OLS", "OLS usually understates the effect because the pile of zeros flattens the slope."),
    ("Sigma", "The standard deviation of the latent error; used in computing marginal effects.")],
   ["Using OLS on the positive observations only; still biased.",
    "Reporting latent coefficients as if they were effects on observed spending.",
    "Using Tobit when the decision to buy and the amount bought are driven by different factors; a two-part model is more appropriate."],
   "Example wording: “The Tobit model (left-censored at 0) showed income had a positive effect on education spending (β = 0.084; P < 0.001). The marginal effect on mean observed spending was 0.052, meaning each additional Rp1 million of income raised average education spending by Rp52 thousand.”",
   [("What is the difference between censored and truncated data?", "Censored: units at the limit remain in the data with their X values. Truncated: units beyond the limit do not enter the sample at all."),
    ("When should a two-part (hurdle) model be used?", "When the decision to participate (zero or positive) and the amount when positive are driven by different factors or directions of effect.")])
